@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+## Context
+
+`copy` in `src/merge-patch.ts` clones objects with `merge({}, value)`, and `merge` deletes null members. See proposal.md - Why.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- `copy` recurses itself: arrays via `map(copy)`, plain objects by building a new object with the existing `set` helper (keeps `__proto__` as an own member) over `Object.keys`, other values returned as is. Alternative `structuredClone` rejected: it would not use the `set` helper's `__proto__` safety.
+- `merge` is unchanged; it still strips nulls only when applying patch objects.
diff --git a/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-fix-merge-patch-copy-keeps-nulls/proposal.md b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-fix-merge-patch-copy-keeps-nulls/proposal.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..41ba04a05b97c80682ea7dd735d4c812cfc08f0e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-fix-merge-patch-copy-keeps-nulls/proposal.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+## Why
+
+`applyMergePatch` strips `null` members from target data the patch does not touch, and from arrays, because its internal deep copy reuses the merge routine, which deletes nulls. This contradicts the spec: unnamed target members are kept and arrays replace wholesale.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- The internal deep copy becomes a plain deep clone that preserves `null` members at every depth, including inside arrays.
+- Only recursive merging of patch objects removes `null` members.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-merge-patch`: adds an explicit requirement that copied data (untouched target members, patch arrays) keeps its `null` members.
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/merge-patch.ts` (`copy`), plus a regression test.
